WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.500
These days, releasing a superhero movie is
a sure-fire way to make a billion dollars

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.250
— but over the years, many have tried and
failed to add to the ever-growing roster of

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:11.820
comic book flicks.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:16.360
These are the most overhyped superhero movies
that never actually happened.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:24.000
The 21st-century superhero film craze began
in earnest in 2002 with Sam Raimi's Spider-Man,

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.330
which starred Tobey Maguire as the masked,
web-spinning crimefighter.

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:33.309
When Raimi's first Spider-venture raked in
an American box office take of $403 million,

00:00:33.309 --> 00:00:36.140
Spider-Man 2 became pretty much inevitable.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.359
That movie did just as well when it was released
in 2004, too, and so Sony naturally followed

00:00:40.359 --> 00:00:45.170
it up with 2007's Spider-Man 3 — a movie
best known for… this.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.269
"How's the pie?"

00:00:47.269 --> 00:00:50.039
"So good."

00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:54.600
Despite attracting so-so reviews, the third
movie still earned more money than both its

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.829
predecessors, meaning Sony was more than justified
in greenlighting Spider-Man 4 and Spider-Man

00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:00.829
5.

00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:05.071
Veteran actors John Malkovich and Anne Hathaway
were both attached to the franchise, and were

00:01:05.071 --> 00:01:10.409
to appear as villain Vulture and love interest
Felicia Hardy, respectively, and the crew

00:01:10.409 --> 00:01:13.530
was supposed to start filming the fourth movie
in early 2010.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:18.619
But in January 2010, just before cameras were
set to roll, Deadline reported that Raimi

00:01:18.619 --> 00:01:22.650
had walked away from the franchise, having
reportedly insisted that a year wasn't enough

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.729
time to make a good enough movie.

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:29.140
Rather than looking for a replacement, Sony
canceled Spider-Man 4 altogether, opting instead

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.430
to reboot the series with a new cast and crew.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:37.689
Sony successfully started their Spider-franchise
anew with 2012's The Amazing Spider-Man, starring

00:01:37.689 --> 00:01:40.909
Andrew Garfield as the world's favorite spider-bite
victim.

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:47.250
The movie made a hugely impressive $757 million
at the worldwide box office, spurring the

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.630
release of a sequel in 2014.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:53.979
Shortly after The Amazing Spider-Man 2 hit
theaters, Sony announced its next big steps

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:56.469
into the Spider-Verse at San Diego Comic-Con.

00:01:56.469 --> 00:02:01.229
November 2016 would see the release of a Sinister
Six movie, debuting the villainous super-team

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:03.530
on screen for the very first time.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:07.890
Consequently, the previously-announced Amazing
Spider-Man 3 was pushed from June 2016 to

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.270
a 2018 release date.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:14.360
But the future of both movies was thrown into
chaos when Sony struck a deal with Marvel

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.911
Studios in 2015, allowing Spider-Man to finally
appear in the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:02:18.911 --> 00:02:20.477
"You got heart, kid.

00:02:20.477 --> 00:02:22.351
Where you from?"

00:02:22.351 --> 00:02:24.144
"Queens."

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:26.201
"Brooklyn."

00:02:26.201 --> 00:02:31.440
The Sony/Marvel co-production Spider-Man:
Homecoming hit theaters in 2017, rebooting

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.720
the franchise yet again with Tom Holland in
the starring role.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.020
The character has only featured in MCU films
since then, while Sony has gone on to develop

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:41.610
its own villain-based Spidey universe.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:48.780
The Superman film franchise lay dormant for
years after the thundering flop that was 1987's

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.040
Superman IV: The Quest for Peace.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.980
That movie earned a genuinely insulting $15
million at the box office, which was just

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.750
less than its already-modest $17 million budget.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:04.230
But in 1996, after Tim Burton's Batman movies
had reinvigorated the superhero movie genre,

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:08.760
Warner Bros.’ Jon Peters brought in filmmaker
Kevin Smith to carry on Superman's story.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:14.070
Smith was supposed to rewrite a screenplay,
then titled Superman Reborn, which was subsequently

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:15.600
renamed Superman Lives.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.260
But Peters had some bizarre demands for Smith.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:23.230
For example, Superman wasn't allowed to fly
in the movie, and he also had to wear an all-black

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:24.230
suit.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:29.370
There were also a number of polar bear bodyguards
involved, for some reason, and at one point

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.230
Supes had to fight a giant mechanical spider.

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:36.180
Smith somehow incorporated all these requirements
into his script, and even recommended that

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.450
his frequent collaborator, Ben Affleck, play
Superman.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:43.760
Tim Burton was hired to direct, however, and
he brought in his own choice to wear the cape:

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.060
Nicolas Cage.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:49.400
Months of rewrites, delays, studio maneuvering,
and budgetary issues ensued, with Superman

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.150
Lives finally kicking the bucket when Burton
stepped down to film Sleepy Hollow instead.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:58.080
The franchise was thrown back into limbo,
and Kal-El wouldn't reappear on the silver

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.430
screen until the release of Superman Returns
in 2006.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.790
Fox churned out a couple of Fantastic Four
movies back in the 2000s: the appropriately

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:11.230
named The Fantastic Four and its follow-up,
Rise of the Silver Surfer.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:15.960
Since that second film made significantly
less money than the first, Fox didn't go for

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:19.910
the full trilogy, but the studio certainly
wasn't done with the characters.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:24.560
The studio hired Green Lantern co-writer Michael
Green to pen a reboot, but three years later

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:26.900
the movie was still stuck in development hell.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:31.060
So Fox brought in Jeremy Slater to write and
hired Josh Trank to direct.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:36.190
But shooting on Fantastic Four was a straight-up
disaster, with Trank alleging multiple instances

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:37.690
of executive meddling.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:41.900
He later told Polygon that his creative input
was often ignored, and that Fox brought in

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:46.790
new writers for reshoots, which the studio
had allegedly planned from the outset.

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:50.820
Scenes that Trank had wanted to film wound
up cut from the script, while other fully-completed

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.510
scenes were left out of the movie's theatrical
cut.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:59.880
The summer 2015 release Fantastic Four didn't
exactly please the fans, earning just $56.1

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:05.620
million in North America and amassing an atrocious
9 percent on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:08.110
"It's fantastic."

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:11.840
"Say that again."

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:18.010
In the wake of that cinematic debacle, Fox
removed their planned Fantastic Four sequel

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.830
from its 2017 release schedule.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:23.850
And now that Marvel Studios are producing
their own Fantastic Four movie, you can probably

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:28.920
safely bet that this particular version of
Marvel's First Family is dead and buried.

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:34.840
Superhero team-up movies are commonplace nowadays,
what with Marvel's hugely successful Avengers

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:38.960
movies and D.C. Comics' slightly-less-successful
Justice League.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.490
But that whole phenomenon might have begun
far earlier if Warner Bros. had seen Justice

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:45.500
League: Mortal to its completion.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:50.280
Production on this superstar superhero movie
began in 2007, with a target release date

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.280
of 2009.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.450
George Miller, the filmmaker behind Mad Max:
Fury Road, agreed to direct, sets were built,

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:59.120
and a cast was hired and fitted for costumes.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.480
Armie Hammer was to star as Batman, D.J.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:06.190
Cotrona was going to play Superman, Megan
Gale would be Wonder Woman, Common came on

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:09.040
as Green Lantern, and Adam Brody was cast
as the Flash.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:14.360
Warner Bros. earmarked an astounding $200
million for Justice League: Mortal, which

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.669
would have ranked it among the most costly
films of all time.

00:06:17.669 --> 00:06:21.300
Unfortunately, the script still needed a good
amount of work, and any potential rewrites

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.790
were delayed by the Writers' Guild of America
strike in 2007 and 2008.

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:29.440
By the time that had been resolved, most of
the cast members' contracts had expired, and

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:31.700
they'd long moved on to other projects.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.900
Movie audiences were introduced to the X-Force
in 2018's Deadpool 2, in which the iconic

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:41.000
super-squad was personally assembled by the
Merc with a Mouth himself.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.850
Unfortunately, it… doesn't end well.

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:49.389
"Oh yeah, that's right, Bedlam."

00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:52.240
[screams]
The X-Force has been around in the comics

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:56.230
in various forms since the early '90s, conceived
as a spinoff of the X-Men.

00:06:56.230 --> 00:07:00.870
Suitably, plans for a now-abandoned X-Force
trilogy were set in motion long before the

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:02.830
Deadpool films were released.

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:07.850
In 2013, Fox hired Kick-Ass 2 filmmaker Jeff
Wadlow to write and direct an X-Force movie.

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:11.120
He later teased the project's premise to ComicBookMovie,
saying:

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:15.970
"If X-Men was about mutants who get to go
to private school with Wolverine and Professor

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:18.280
X, what about the mutants that have to go
to public school?

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:21.680
What about the ones who don't have the benefactor
looking out for them?"

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:25.600
Wadlow apparently developed a story that would
unfold over three movies, following a band

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:29.170
of kids who eventually form a hit squad and
black-ops team.

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:33.490
Now that Disney has acquired Fox, however,
it seems the chances of an X-Force movie happening

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:36.680
anytime soon have flown straight into the
woodchipper.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:41.510
After the X-Men and the Avengers, it seemed
like the next big thing in the world of Marvel

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:43.760
was destined to be the Inhumans.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.530
The studio whetted the public's appetite for
the lesser-known team of X-Men-like individuals,

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:53.320
first by introducing them to the MCU on ABC's
popular series Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D., and

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:56.419
then by announcing plans to spin them off
into their own movie.

00:07:56.419 --> 00:08:01.280
At a Marvel media event in 2014, studio boss
Kevin Feige said that Inhumans would mark

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:07.080
the 20th MCU entry, comprising a huge part
of the studio's "Phase 3" slate of films.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:10.199
It was due to hit theaters on November 2nd,
2018.

00:08:10.199 --> 00:08:12.860
Then it was suddenly delayed until summer
2019.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:14.900
And then Marvel delayed the project indefinitely.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:15.900
The reason?

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:19.901
Well, when Marvel made a deal with Sony to
include Peter Parker in the MCU, the studio

00:08:19.901 --> 00:08:23.750
pivoted its attention towards their burgeoning
Spider-Man franchise.

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:28.080
Considering the Inhumans were second-tier
at best compared to Spidey, the project was

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:32.979
quickly shelved in his place — and eventually
turned into a widely-panned and quickly canceled

00:08:32.979 --> 00:08:34.869
ABC television series.

00:08:35.869 --> 00:08:39.699
Long before Marvel Comics realized it could
make its own movies in-house, the publisher

00:08:39.699 --> 00:08:43.909
would contract with various different movie
studios to bring its most popular superheroes

00:08:43.909 --> 00:08:45.149
to life on the big screen.

00:08:45.149 --> 00:08:49.980
While Fox wound up with the rights to the
X-Men and its assorted characters, for example,

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:52.930
Universal Pictures got the rights to make
an Incredible Hulk movie.

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:57.389
Production on Hulk began in 2002, starring
Australian actor Eric Bana as the brilliant

00:08:57.389 --> 00:09:01.350
but troubled scientist Bruce Banner — and
it was all under the direction of the acclaimed

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.699
and award-winning filmmaker Ang Lee.

00:09:03.699 --> 00:09:07.930
Marvel was so optimistic that Hulk would be
a hit that it contracted with Universal to

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:10.079
make a couple more Marvel movies.

00:09:10.079 --> 00:09:12.750
Marvel Studios' CEO Avi Arad said at the time:

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:17.029
"Universal Pictures has proven to us that
it has the vision and creativity to bring

00:09:17.029 --> 00:09:20.240
our superheroes to the big screen in grand
fashion."

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.779
He subsequently announced an upcoming "epic
underwater tale of majestic fantasy."

00:09:24.779 --> 00:09:30.249
No, not that one — this film was actually
Sub-Mariner, a story about the aquatic anti-hero

00:09:30.249 --> 00:09:33.029
and classic Marvel character Namor.

00:09:33.029 --> 00:09:36.910
Screenwriter David Self, best known at the
time for Road to Perdition, was attached to

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:37.910
write the screenplay.

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:42.119
Apparently, the story was to follow the protector
of Atlantis as he discovers his deep-sea powers

00:09:42.119 --> 00:09:44.559
and wages war against the evil Krang.

00:09:44.559 --> 00:09:48.970
Sub-Mariner failed to come up for air, however,
and after the project bounced between a number

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:52.809
of other filmmakers, the rights to the character
eventually reverted back to Marvel.

00:09:52.809 --> 00:09:56.380
As of right now, the character has yet to
make his silver screen debut.

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:01.959
At the same time that a Hulk-inspired contracting
frenzy led Marvel to ink their deal with Universal,

00:10:01.959 --> 00:10:06.579
the two entertainment behemoths announced
another movie based on Prime, a relatively

00:10:06.579 --> 00:10:09.899
obscure comic book character dating back to
the early 1990s.

00:10:09.899 --> 00:10:11.869
Avi Arad announced at the time:

00:10:11.869 --> 00:10:15.819
"Prime is a complete departure from the standard
superhero story.

00:10:15.819 --> 00:10:21.470
With this film, we are developing what we
think will be Marvel's first superhero action-comedy."

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:25.940
Marvel big shots Kevin Feige and Stan Lee
were cited as executive producers, while Arad

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:28.079
was set to actively produce the film.

00:10:28.079 --> 00:10:33.040
It was supposed to tell an extremely Shazam-like
tale of a teenager who, as the result of a

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:37.949
government experiment, could instantly turn
into an adult — who's also a superhero.

00:10:37.949 --> 00:10:41.680
Screenwriting duo Don Calame and Chris Conroy
were responsible for the script, even though

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:46.329
they had just one feature film credit between
them: the 2001 Disney Channel original movie

00:10:46.329 --> 00:10:47.559
Hounded.

00:10:47.559 --> 00:10:52.339
Seeing as how no progress has been made on
this film in nearly two decades, it's probably

00:10:52.339 --> 00:10:54.899
fair to say that the time for Prime has passed.

00:10:55.899 --> 00:11:00.749
In late 2018, Deadline reported that Fox had
hired Julius Avery, director of the horror

00:11:00.749 --> 00:11:04.660
movie Overlord, to helm a new, big-budget
film version of Flash Gordon.

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:09.540
A popular comic strip in the early 20th century,
it centered on the exploits of muscular American

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:13.949
athlete Flash Gordon, who heads into space
to fight the evil Ming the Merciless.

00:11:13.949 --> 00:11:18.949
The last time it appeared on the big screen
was as a campy low-budget movie in 1980, starring

00:11:18.949 --> 00:11:24.420
Sam J. Jones as Flash and featuring an iconic
soundtrack by Queen.

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:26.160
"Gordon's alive?!"

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:31.019
After Kingsman filmmaker Matthew Vaughn opted
to produce but not direct the movie, Fox accepted

00:11:31.019 --> 00:11:35.440
a story pitch from Avery, who is a lifelong
fan of the Flash Gordon comic strip.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:39.779
That version of the movie ultimately fell
apart, but shortly after Disney acquired Fox

00:11:39.779 --> 00:11:45.050
in 2019, Thor: Ragnarok director Taika Waititi
was announced as the new director of the long-gestating

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:49.379
Flash Gordon revival, which had apparently
been reworked as an animated feature.

00:11:49.379 --> 00:11:53.050
Not more than a few weeks later, however,
Flash Gordon appeared on a long list of projects

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:57.640
in various states of pre-production that Disney
had inherited from Fox... and which the Mouse

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:59.759
House had summarily cancelled.

00:11:59.759 --> 00:12:02.579
Ming isn't the only merciless one, it would
seem.

00:12:03.579 --> 00:12:08.040
For decades now, Sony has controlled all of
Spider-Man's many villains and supporting

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:11.390
characters, and a few years ago the studio
decided to tell their stories as part of a

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:12.550
shared universe.

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:17.429
Now officially titled the Sony Pictures Universe
of Marvel's Characters, this wide-ranging

00:12:17.429 --> 00:12:22.239
franchise includes every Spider-Verse move
that isn't specifically centered on Spider-Man,

00:12:22.239 --> 00:12:26.820
such as the Tom Hardy vehicle Venom and its
sequel, as well as Morbius, starring Jared

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:29.179
Leto as the titular "Living Vampire."

00:12:29.179 --> 00:12:33.110
Sony also planned a team-up movie featuring
two other Spider-Man characters — the mercenary

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:36.149
Silver Sable and the ace cat burglar Black
Cat.

00:12:36.149 --> 00:12:40.689
In May 2017, Sony tapped Gina Prince-Bythewood
to direct Silver and Black, with plans for

00:12:40.689 --> 00:12:43.139
a theatrical release date of February 2019.

00:12:43.139 --> 00:12:48.009
That all changed in June 2018, however, when
Sony took the movie off of its schedule.

00:12:48.009 --> 00:12:51.649
There were apparently internal problems with
the production — specifically that Prince-Bythewood

00:12:51.649 --> 00:12:54.930
reportedly didn't much care for the shooting
script she'd been given.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:58.540
Continuous delays over the script meant that
meeting a theatrical release eight months

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:00.749
later would have been next to impossible.

00:13:00.749 --> 00:13:05.139
However, it wasn't long after scrapping Silver
and Black that Sony decided it would instead

00:13:05.139 --> 00:13:08.779
pursue separate movies for the two characters,
both of which are currently in development…

00:13:08.779 --> 00:13:09.779
for now.

00:13:10.779 --> 00:13:15.870
James Franco is an artist with many passions,
from writing to directing to painting — and

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:19.000
he isn't above showing up in the odd superhero
movie, either.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:23.040
Early in his acting career, he played Harry
Osborn in Sam Raimi's 2000s Spider-Man trilogy.

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:27.560
Not only that, he nearly became one of the
few actors to play more than one comic book

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:31.610
character in different franchises, having
been attached to star as the mutant Multiple

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:33.910
Man in a standalone film for Fox.

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:38.570
In 2017, Deadline reported that the man of
multiple talents would both produce and star

00:13:38.570 --> 00:13:44.309
in a film about the mutant James Madrox, who
is able to create clones of himself at will.

00:13:44.309 --> 00:13:48.920
Franco told The Hollywood Reporter in 2017
that he wanted to make a different, edgier

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:50.949
comic book movie, akin to Deadpool or Logan.

00:13:50.949 --> 00:13:51.949
He said:

00:13:51.949 --> 00:13:55.869
"Our bottom line MO is, how can we push this
into new ground?

00:13:55.869 --> 00:13:59.799
We're going to take this superhero thing and
push it into a new genre."

00:13:59.799 --> 00:14:04.390
X-Men franchise overseer Simon Kinberg was
also actively involved in the project, and

00:14:04.390 --> 00:14:08.980
nearly a year after its announcement, he told
IGN that a script was currently being worked

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:09.980
on.

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:13.960
That, of course, was in 2018 — and with
Disney having acquired Fox soon after, it's

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:16.220
definitely not looking good for Multiple Man.

00:14:17.509 --> 00:14:23.119
There are three things that almost everyone
can agree are fantastic: cowboys, ninjas,

00:14:23.119 --> 00:14:24.600
and vikings.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:29.560
Most filmgoers are also of the consensus that
Chris Pratt is awesome, as the likable actor

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:34.360
has headlined blockbusters as disparate as
Jurassic World, Guardians of the Galaxy, and

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:35.509
The Lego Movie.

00:14:35.509 --> 00:14:39.480
Hollywood producers searching for a surefire
hit didn't have to think too hard, then, to

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:44.379
combine cowboys, ninjas, Vikings, and Chris
Pratt via the movie Cowboy Ninja Viking.

00:14:44.379 --> 00:14:47.050
Based on the graphic novel of the same name
by A.J.

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:51.600
Lieberman and Riley Rossmo, the film was to
depict the mind-blowing adventures of Dr.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:55.730
Sebastian Ghislain, a psychotherapist who
can transform into three different secret

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:59.079
agent personas — a cowboy, a ninja, and
a Viking.

00:14:59.079 --> 00:15:03.339
Pratt signed on to star as Dr. Ghislain, with
Priyanka Chopra featuring in a supporting

00:15:03.339 --> 00:15:04.339
role.

00:15:04.339 --> 00:15:08.089
Game of Thrones director Michelle MacLaren
would make her feature film debut with a $65

00:15:08.089 --> 00:15:10.009
million budget to play with.

00:15:10.009 --> 00:15:14.699
This fun action comedy was scheduled to hit
movie theaters on June 28th, 2019, but just

00:15:14.699 --> 00:15:18.949
a week after Chopra's involvement was announced,
Universal pulled the movie from its schedule

00:15:18.949 --> 00:15:22.110
and ended production, delaying the film indefinitely.

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:26.319
According to an insider, the film had a number
of script issues still to work out.

00:15:26.319 --> 00:15:30.860
Its problems were apparently so bad, in fact,
that rumors Universal would cancel the movie

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:35.980
had floated around for nearly half a year
before the studio finally called it a day.

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:40.529
Over the course of the last two decades, the
X-Men series has been one of Hollywood's most

00:15:40.529 --> 00:15:43.300
lucrative and expansive franchises.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:49.069
The dozen-plus films in the series raked in
more than $6 billion altogether, and Fox explored

00:15:49.069 --> 00:15:54.610
all kinds of offshoots and sub-franchises,
such as Hugh Jackman's Wolverine standalones.

00:15:54.610 --> 00:16:00.790
These included the instant classic Logan,
the less-beloved The Wolverine, and the much-derided

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:02.470
X-Men Origins: Wolverine.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:06.359
The studio also toyed with other Origins movies,
however — such as one about the rise of

00:16:06.359 --> 00:16:11.309
super-villain Magneto, which was later reworked
into the team-up prequel X-Men: First Class.

00:16:11.309 --> 00:16:16.089
They also considered a film about Remy LeBeau,
otherwise known as Gambit, the Louisiana-born

00:16:16.089 --> 00:16:18.889
mutant with a thing for explosive playing
cards.

00:16:18.889 --> 00:16:22.600
Gambit has made a few appearances in X-Men-related
media over the years.

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:27.559
He was a main character on Fox's 1990s X-Men
animated series, and he showed up for a cameo

00:16:27.559 --> 00:16:30.410
in X-Men Origins: Wolverine.

00:16:30.410 --> 00:16:37.089
"Now, bub, you listen to me."

00:16:37.089 --> 00:16:41.179
When announcing a solo Gambit film, however,
Fox ignored Taylor Kitsch, who played the

00:16:41.179 --> 00:16:44.029
character in that film, in favor of 21 Jump
Street and G.I.

00:16:44.029 --> 00:16:45.489
Joe star Channing Tatum.

00:16:45.489 --> 00:16:49.399
A succession of high-profile directors circled
the movie, including The Bourne Identity's

00:16:49.399 --> 00:16:52.670
Doug Liman and Pirates of the Caribbean's
Gore Verbinski.

00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:56.769
Somehow, though, Fox just couldn't get this
movie off the ground, and the studio delayed

00:16:56.769 --> 00:17:01.810
its release once or twice in 2019, before
settling on March 2020.

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:06.360
After Disney acquired Fox, the House of Mouse
quietly canceled the movie, finally consigning

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:08.390
it to the ash heap of Hollywood history.

